content.text:
Found it: `.lg-surface { position: relative; }` is **unlayered** (layer ""), so it beats all layered rules including utilities. That's the bug! `.lg-surface` at line 617 outside @layer components. So any `.lg-surface` (nav, search, etc.) is forced relative. This breaks sticky nav and likely other positioning. This is a significant CSS issue.

Fix: scope `.lg-surface` position relative only when needed, or remove it. `.lg-surface` is applied to nav (sticky), search (static, doesn't need relative because `.glass-search` base already position relative), panels (need relative for canvas host), dock (fixed), orb (fixed), chat (maybe fixed). The `.lg-surface` rule exists to establish positioning context for `.lg-canvas-host` and `.lg-tint`. But if the element has `position: sticky` or `fixed`, it's already a containing block, so no need. If it's static (e.g., login panel? `glass-panel lg-surface`), the `.liquid-glass` base rule gives `position: relative` (layered components) — but `.lg-surface` unlayered was ensuring it. Could change `.lg-surface` to:
```
.lg-surface:not(.sticky):not(.fixed) { position: relative; }
```
But that still unlayered and beats other position utilities (absolute?). Better move `.lg-surface` into `@layer components` so utilities win. It's a component class; should be layered. Then `.sticky` wins for nav. `.lg-surface` relative remains for elements without position utilities. This is the correct fix. But glass.css's WebGL section is outside `@layer components`; we can wrap `.lg-surface` rule in `@layer components { ... }` or add `@layer components { .lg-surface { ... } }` around the block. However many WebGL rules outside layers intentionally override inline styles/utilities (e.g., `html[data-glass-engine=webgl] ... !important`). We only need `.lg-surface` position rule layered. Change:
```
@layer components {
  .lg-surface { position: relative; isolation: isolate; }
}
```
But `isolation: isolate` is also useful; utilities don't conflict. Put both in layer. `.lg-surface` is a component class, so layer is appropriate. This will let `sticky` and `fixed` utilities win. Great.

Then nav becomes sticky and our scroll optimization works. This also fixes the "scroll delay" perception? The nav disappearing was maybe not noticed. But now fixed nav glass must repaint smoothly; our 60fps anchored path will handle it.

Also check `.lg-canvas-host` unlayered `position: absolute !important` etc. fine. `.lg-tint` unlayered absolute; fine.

Let's edit glass.css `.lg-surface` block.

